not many people have looked at what  we're not really talking about very much  on any level is about teacher well-being  and what kind of mental state teachers  need to be in to teach to the best of  their abilities and what we know is that  if the teacher is in the right place  mentally if they feel good about what  they're doing they're gonna teach better  they will be more creative if they'll be  more motivated they'll be more engaged  it's actually starts off this sort of  positive cycle that if the teacher is in  the right place mentally there's a very  good chance - learners will be - so one  of the things that I'm keen to stress is  that looking after teacher well-being  making teacher well being a priority  understanding teacher psychology  shouldn't be an optional extra shouldn't  be considered some kind of luxury it's  actually fundamental to what it means to  be a good teacher for a lot of teachers  here and they'll be saying hey that's  great but how do I look after myself  there's so many constraints as them  because it's my time there's so many  other things I need to be thinking about  whatever how what can I do to make sure  that I'm looking often myself as a as a  teacher I mean I think one of the first  things that we all have to decide for  ourselves is what our priorities are in  life and this means really taking stock  of what's important to me in terms of  life goal and professional goals so one  of the things that is perhaps  misunderstood and oversimplified is this  idea of work-life balance we have this  idea that there's some kind of fixed  proportion of work and life balance that  we should be aiming for and that's just  not true everybody has to find what  works for them and particularly for  teachers it's not usually that sort of  strict separation of work and  professional life they're very blurred  it means by starting to sort of say what  am i value for what's important to me  where what am I satisfied with looking  at the different areas of my life so my  intellectual well-being my emotional  well-being my spiritual well-being and  my physical well-being and and then  really having to sort of do an audit and  say how how much am i attending to this  and does that match up with my  priorities do I feel comfortable with  that do other places I need to make  adjustments whatever job we're doing we  all find ourselves in the position where  there's way too much to do and not  enough time so I think the first thing  we have to do is we have to sort of  brutally set priorities and we  to learn to say no to things we have to  learn that some things will not get done  but it's making the decisions about what  those things are and saying they have to  go okay about how do you make those  decisions only you can do that mmm could  you give an example of maybe over way in  which you kind of were able to make  those perfect those and I think one of  the things I've learned is that you have  to keep trying to do it you don't get to  a point where I have the perfect  work-life balance now I'm happy with  everything as if I don't need to do  anything I make sure that I take  holidays and I book it off when I'm on  holiday I'm really on holiday that you  really switch off one of the things that  that's coming out of research is this  concept of detachment is really being a  hundred percent in that moment of not  being in work mode I think that the big  thing is making the time when you're not  at work  quality time the other thing and that  also comes from some of the research  we've been doing positive psychology is  really learning to understand what's  within my control and what's not if I  can control it I just let it go and I  feel very strongly that if I can start  to see the positives in everything  that's around you there's a lot of  research that your well-being is  influenced by positive thoughts and  positive well-being so they teach you  for example to do gratitude gratitude  where and I have been doing this for  about a year now and I find it very  helpful is at the end of the day I need  to take a minute you know literally  before I go to sleep I just think of all  the things in the day that I have to be  grateful for and that went well  then well so it's very nice and and I  tell you every day there were so many  nice things that happen and there are  things that you are grateful for  and the more you start to think of it  the more things you become aware of any  such is very easy to take note of so  from what I've understood so far if I  were to make a list okay here's you me  okay yeah I'd say prioritize absolutely  make sure that the time that you have  off work is off work so quality time and  thinking about what you're grateful  about after finding a positive your life  finding the positives in your life  finding the positives in your job and  it's not to deny the negatives and  they're things you'll have to cope and  deal with but it's learning that there's  a lot of positive things and sometimes  we take them for granted  being coming consciously aware of them  can can remind of actually there's a lot  of very good things going on as well  what would be specific to a teacher  maybe I think it applies yeah you're  right I think a lot this applies  generally to people who are working in  all kinds of professions and I think  this this problem with work/life balance  though is perhaps particularly acute for  teachers because there is a very  blurring between work and private life  we're very dedicated it's very much part  of our life and part of our identity so  very often we define ourselves through  our work more so than in some other  professions although other professions  have this as well and so I think  sometimes finding this distinction and  finding how to manage this balance  between these these different areas of  our lives is very difficult and I think  teaching is inherently a stressful job I  think teachers tend to be very other  oriented so they tend to give a lot and  feel guilty for paying attention to  themselves and I think that is also a  problem is that talking about your own  well-being talking about your own needs  talking about looking after yourself and  it's something that sometimes teachers  feel guilty for because they're so other  oriented they're so oriented to helping  their learner's and being there and  while that's always going to be a  priority for us if we start to  understand that if we look after  ourselves first we are better able to be  there for our learners so it's not  selfish at all it's actually just good  practice
 

